[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked **known**: one issue, the parser, one fix queued. Read the
reports instead of their subject lines and it holds <Count ticket={4471} of="problems" /> problems,
each with its own cause, its own fix and now its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. The queued parser fix closes the <Count ticket={4471} of="parser" /> parser reports.
The <Count ticket={4471} of="hint" /> hint reports and the <Count ticket={4471} of="one-day" /> one-day
reports have a different cause and stay broken after it ships.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {problems} />

## What each one is

### `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

Every tenant on file writes the day first. The shared parser reads every date month first, so day
and month swap: a booking for 7 April goes out as 4 July, 2 December becomes 12 February. Reports
come from every tenant and every way in: app, email and booking-v2 forms. This is the fix #4471
already queued. Ticket: <a href={problems[0].ticket}>parser</a>.

### `hint`: the booking-v3 form tells people to type the month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. At a day-first tenant people type what the
hint says, month first, and the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first. The symptom is the
same swapped date, so the subject lines match the parser reports, but the code is different: the
parser fix does not touch it. Only reports that came in through the form of a booking-v3 tenant
(alder.physio, copper.studio, pale.harbour). The fix is the hint: print the tenant's own order.
Ticket: <a href={problems[1].ticket}>hint</a>.

### `one-day`: bookings land one day early

Nothing is swapped. Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday 11 May; Tuesday 6 October shows as Monday; a
client in Lisbon got Tuesday and a client in Toronto got Monday for the same meeting; every booking
in June is a day off; an invite arrived before the booking was made. A date is being moved across a
time zone. The desk already read these as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`), but the cause table
on #4471 still files them under `parser` by tenant, so they never had a ticket. The parser fix does
not touch them either. Ticket: <a href={problems[2].ticket}>one-day</a>.

## How the reports were counted

1. **One id, one report.** The ticket export comes in overlapping pages:
   <Count ticket={4471} of="rows" /> rows, <Count ticket={4471} of="repeats" /> of them repeats,
   <Count ticket={4471} of="reports" /> reports.
2. **Cause from the tenant, not from intake.** `causes.ts` decides `parser` or `hint` from the
   tenant's locale, its booking form and the way the report came in. Intake's tag is a guess from
   the reporter's browser: it tags every `en-US` browser `hint`, whatever the tenant runs.
3. **One-day moves on their own.** Reports listed in `desk-notes.json` as a one-day move are the
   `one-day` problem, wherever their tenant would put them.
4. **Every report in exactly one problem.** The table checks that the problems add up to the ticket.

All numbers on this page are computed at render time from the same data as the #4471 page
(`problems.ts`, `ProblemTable`, `ProblemCount`), so they follow the export as the desk adds to it.

## Why they ended up in one ticket

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports whose subject lines match. All
the problems produce the same subjects ("Wrong day on the invite", "Month and day swapped?"), so
the subject cannot tell them apart, and "when unsure, merge" put everything in #4471. Matching on
cause (`causes.ts`, as the [triage explorer](/kb/triage/dedupe) already does) would have kept them
apart. That change needs a new decision record from its deciders; 0001 stays as accepted.

